      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine how far Jana will walk in 10 minutes given that she takes 24 minutes to walk one mile, we can set up a proportion based on her walking rate.\n\nFirst, we know that 24 minutes is equivalent to 1 mile walked. We want to find out how far she will walk in 10 minutes.\n\nWe can set up the problem as a ratio where the number of minutes is the numerator and the distance is the denominator. Since 1 mile corresponds to 24 minutes, we can write the ratio as follows:\n\n\\[ \\text{Time}_\\text{walked} = 10 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Distance} = x \\text{ miles} \\]\n\nThe relationship can be given by:\n\n\\[ \\frac{24 \\text{ minutes}}{1 \\text{ mile}} = \\frac{10 \\text{ minutes}}{x \\text{ miles}} \\]\n\nNow, we solve for \\( x \\) (the distance in miles). Cross-multiplying gives us:\n\n\\[ 24 \\times x = 10 \\times 1 \\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[ 24x = 10 \\]\n\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{10}{24} \\]\n\nFinally, we reduce the fraction:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{10}{24} = \\frac{5}{12} \\]\n\nConverting the fraction to a decimal:\n\n\\[ x \\approx 0.4166666666666667 \\]\n\nRounded to the nearest tenth:\n\n\\[ x \\approx 0.4 \\]\n\nSo, Jana will walk approximately $\\boxed{0.4}$ miles in 10 minutes.",
